--- IR --->
# a literal is still a *constant* with the whole library behind it - static bytes, a length and a
# null owner. `literal_is_a_string.test` makes this claim about a hand-declared type; this one makes
# it about the stdlib's, which is the one every program actually gets
#
# the llvm struct name is deliberately not spelled: the stdlib compilation unit declares `%string`
# first, so the main unit's copy of the same Echo type is uniquified to `%string.N`. what matters is
# the shape, and the shape is the tail of the line
CHECK: define i32 @main(
CHECK: { ptr @str, i64 5 }, ptr null, i64 0, i64 0 }

# and getting all of that costs nothing. no allocation for the bytes, and no reference count moved -
# the owner is null and both operations are null-guarded. scoped to main on purpose: the allocation seam
# is defined at module level and `->clone()` calls it, neither of which this program reaches
CHECK-NOT: call ptr @__eco_alloc
CHECK-NOT: strong.inc

CHECK: ret i32 0
